{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And He said.</b> When it has a segol under a small pataḥ [pathaḥ], its meaning is mimmaʾenu [from us], and if it has a qamatz gadol, it would be in construct state, and likewise כְּאַחַד שבטי ישראל (Genesis 49:16). Therefore it is not possible, by grammatical analysis, for its interpretation to be like eḥad [one], and what sense would it have? And the master of the accents was right to join mimmaʾenu [from us] with ladaʿat [to know]," "And mimmaʾenu is a plural form, like ish mimmenu (Genesis 23:6). I have already explained in the Book of the Foundation why the nun of the plural takes a dagesh, and the people of the East who pronounce it without a dagesh err. And the sense of the verse is like 'you shall be like Elohim, knowers of good and evil,' or the interpretation concerns His thought. And do not wonder at the word mimmaʾenu, for its parallel is 'Let us make man in our image' (Genesis 26:1) [— Genesis 1:26], 'Let us go down' (Genesis 11:7), and this is the speech of HaShem with the angels:"], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {0 "The text reads 'פתח קטן/גדול' (small/great pataḥ); 'segol under a small pataḥ' renders what appears to be a corrupted or abbreviated description of the vowel pointing on ממנו.", 1 "The verse citation 'Genesis 26:1' is erroneous in the source; the quoted phrase is from Genesis 1:26. Translated hyper-literally as printed."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:43:08.503086161Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}